{"type": "message", "id": "86b61edd", "parentId": "4fa300e7", "timestamp": "2026-04-07T14:44:14.827Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have all the quarterly data from Tesla's SEC filings (10-Qs and 10-K):\n\n**Q1 2023 (period ended March 31, 2023):**\n- Total revenues: $23,329 million\n- Net Income (GAAP consolidated): $2,539 million\n- Net profit margin = 2,539 / 23,329 × 100 = 10.88%\n\n**Q2 2023 (period ended June 30, 2023):**\n- Total revenues: $24,927 million\n- Net Income (GAAP consolidated): $2,614 million\n\n\n- Net profit margin = 2,614 / 24,927 × 100 = 10.49%\n\nFor Q3 2023, the net profit margin dropped to 7.83% as revenues stayed relatively flat while net income declined. Q4 2023 shows a significant spike with net income jumping to $7,943 million, though the data appears incomplete.\n\nNow I'm applying the formula as stated in the question—dividing total net income by total revenues and multiplying by 100—which gives Q4 2023 an exceptionally high margin of 31.56% due to a one-time tax benefit of $5.9 billion. This is what the GAAP figures show, so using the exact formula requested, only Q1 and Q4 2023 exceed the 10.5% threshold. Q2 2023 falls just short at 10.49% when I round it out—close enough that it's clearly below the 10.5% threshold. So my final answer is Q1 and Q4.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Now I have all the data from Tesla's SEC filings (10-Qs and 10-K). Let me compile and calculate:\n\n**Q1 2023** (ended March 31, 2023): Net Income = $2,539M, Revenue = $23,329M → Margin = **10.88%**\n**Q2 2023** (ended June 30, 2023): Net Income = $2,614M, Revenue = $24,927M → Margin = **10.49%**\n**Q3 2023** (ended Sept 30, 2023): Net Income = $1,878M, Revenue = $23,350M → Margin = **7.93%**\n**Q4 2023** (ended Dec 31, 2023): Net Income = $7,943M, Revenue = $25,167M → Margin = **31.56%**"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_3f88b992f1af41eba02c6d6b", "name": "write", "arg